  "summary": "This paper presents a finite-population resampling benchmark evaluating whether revisiting reference locations (paired sampling) and incorporating model-assisted predictions from frozen satellite foundation-model embeddings (TESSERA and AlphaEarth Foundations) improve the estimation of annual changes in crop-group Shannon diversity across 30 held-out Swiss landscapes (90 adjacent-year transitions from 2022 to 2025 in SwissCrop25). At an illustrative budget of 128 reference labels per year, direct revisited sampling achieves a 0.275 mean squared error (MSE) ratio relative to independent sampling overall (0.198 in grassland-dominated landscapes and 0.597 in arable-dominated landscapes), though on stable arable support crop rotation can induce negative pixel-level influence covariance that eliminates the pairing advantage. Critically, because true annual landscape entropy changes are small (RMSE 0.0260 for predicting zero change), a simple no-change baseline outperforms direct sampling at all tested budgets (32 to 512 labels/year) and beats all model-assisted estimators except revisited random-forest assistance at 512 labels/year. Furthermore, the paper diagnoses two important interval-estimation pitfalls: (1) direct revisited Wald intervals undercover (83.4% vs. 93.1% independent) largely because error-free database repeatability yields zero observed label changes—and thus degenerate zero-width intervals—in 12.9% of draws, and (2) the prespecified model-assisted delta-method intervals appeared overly conservative (~99% coverage) only because they omitted the Jacobian of the Euclidean probability-simplex projection; propagating the derivative through the projection narrows standard errors by roughly half and brings coverage to ~93.6%–94.1%.",
